Your commas inside quoted strings mean `-F,` will split fields incorrectly, so `$9` is not reliably the date field.

A closer fit is to split on the quoted separator instead, for example `-F'","'`, then adjust the date field. The suggested approach in the thread is:

awk -F'","' 'BEGIN {
  split("JAN FEB MAR APR MAY JUN JUL AUG SEP OCT NOV DEC", month, " ")
  for (i=1; i<=12; i++) mdigit[month[i]]=i
}
{
  if (NR==1) { print; next }
  m=substr($9,4,3)
  $9=sprintf("%02d/%02d/20%02d", mdigit[m], substr($9,1,2), substr($9,8,2))
  print
}' OFS="," file.csv

This only helps if your CSV is consistently quoted as shown.

Yes. You can prepend the target path directly in `find`’s `-exec`, and you don’t need to remove the leading `.`:

find . -exec mv {} /mountedSystem/{} \;

This works because `/./` in the middle of a path has no effect, so `./home/usr1/dir1/file` becomes `/mountedSystem/./home/usr1/dir1/file`, which is equivalent to `/mountedSystem/home/usr1/dir1/file`.

Read the file line by line, keep the needed part from the current record, and apply it to the next records before writing them out. A Perl approach is:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my ($part1, $part2);

while (my $line = <>) {
    chomp $line;

    if ($line =~ /^MTD.{8}(...)(.*?) /) {
        $part1 = $1;
        $part2 = $2;
        substr($line, 11, 3, $part1);
    }

    if ($line =~ /^([AB])\1 /) {
        substr($line, 11, 0, $part1 . $part2);
    }

    print "$line\n";
}
```

This keeps the relevant string from an `MTD` line and inserts it into the following `AA` or `BB` lines, then writes the transformed result to another file.

