One way is to use Perl in paragraph mode to read each block, collect the values by the first column, then print the rows sorted numerically by that first field.

The accepted solution does it with a script like this:

#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my %final_lines;
my $filename = shift // die "No input file given!\n";
open my $IN,'<',$filename or die "Failed to open $filename for input: $!\n";

local $/="";
while(<$IN>){
    my @lines  = split /\n/;
    my @fields = split /\s+/,(shift @lines);
    my $line_number = 0;
    for my $line (@lines){
        my @data = split /\s+/,$line;
        map { $final_lines{$fields[$_]}->[$line_number] += $data[$_] } (0..$#data);
        $line_number++;
    }
}

for my $key (sort { $a <=> $b } keys %final_lines){
    local $,=' ';
    print $key, @{$final_lines{$key}}, "\n";
}

Run it as:

/path/to/script input_file > output_file

It transposes the columns into rows and then sorts by the first column.

If your file has a regular column structure like the example, GNU awk can transpose it:

awk '{for (j = 1; j <= NF; ++j) arr[NR,j] = $j};
END{
  for (j=1; j <= NF; j+=2)
    for (i=1; i <= NR; ++i){
      printf i == 1 ? "" : " "
      printf arr[i,j] " " arr[i,j+1]
      if (i == NR) printf "\n"
    }
}' input.txt

This groups two columns per output row, as shown in the example. If you want a simpler bash approach, the post also shows a `paste` + `cut` solution.

